## Stripe Setup

Follow these steps to set up Stripe for your SaaS application:

### 1. Install the Stripe CLI

First, install the Stripe CLI on your machine. Follow the instructions here: https://stripe.com/docs/stripe-cli 

You can verify the installation by running:

```bash
stripe --version
```

### 2. Create Products and Subscriptions in Test Mode

#### 2.1. Create a Product

Log in to your [Stripe Dashboard (Test Mode)](https://dashboard.stripe.com/test/products)  
Create a new **Product** (e.g., "Pro Plan") that your users will subscribe to.

#### 2.2. Add a Pricing Plan (for Subscription)

- While creating or after creating your Product, add a **Recurring** pricing plan (e.g., $10/month).  
  This will allow you to create actual subscriptions to this product.

> ℹ️ A **Product** in Stripe is what you are selling (e.g., "Pro Plan").  
> A **Price** represents how you bill for that product (e.g., recurring $10/month for a subscription).

### 3. Connect Stripe CLI to Your Local Webhook Endpoint

Open a terminal and run:

```bash
stripe listen --forward-to localhost:3000/api/stripe/webhook
```

This will create a webhook in your **Stripe Test mode** and forward all Stripe events locally.  
Keep this process running when developing locally.

You will be given a webhook secret (starts with `whsec_...`). Make sure to copy this value to your `.env` file as `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET`.

### 4. Testing Payments

Use Stripe test card numbers (like `4242 4242 4242 4242`) to test your checkout and subscription flows.

### 5. Setting up the Production Webhook

When you deploy your app, you need to create a separate webhook in your **Stripe Live/Production mode** that points to your production server. To do this:

1. Go to your [Stripe Dashboard (Live mode)](https://dashboard.stripe.com/webhooks).
2. Click "Add endpoint."
3. Enter your production webhook endpoint, e.g.  
   ```
   https://yourdomain.com/api/stripe/webhook
   ```
4. Select the events you want to listen for (e.g., `checkout.session.completed`, `invoice.paid`, `customer.subscription.updated`, etc).
5. Save the endpoint.
6. Copy the webhook secret (another string like `whsec_...`) and set it as `STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET` in your production environment variables.

> ⚠️ Make sure you use the **correct keys and webhook secrets** for test and live environments.

---

Now Stripe is set up for both test and production!
